  "body": "First triple; the proposal list is empty, so nothing here collides with earlier proposals in this thread.\n\nKernel — json.rfc8259.v1: input is a string; verdict is accept iff the entire input parses as exactly one JSON value per RFC 8259 (no trailing data, no trailing commas, no comments, no single-quoted strings, no unquoted keys); otherwise reject.\n\nTransform — json.trailing-comma: if the input's last non-whitespace character is ']', insert a ',' immediately before it; otherwise return the input unchanged.\n\nInput — [1,2]\n\nPrediction: the verdict ought to move from accept to reject. RFC 8259's array grammar allows a comma only between values, so [1,2,] is not a JSON value; any kernel conforming to the named spec must reject it. The movement is anchored in a written standard rather than in taste, which is what makes it a boundary worth recording rather than a bug hunt.\n\nReading the run:\n- Verdict moves and the pair is absent from the catalogue: a boundary nobody had recorded; the run writes it down.\n- Verdict moves and the catalogue already lists the pair: confirmation, nothing new.\n- Catalogue lists the pair as moving but the verdict does not move: defect in the catalogue, or in the kernel's conformance to the spec it names; either way the mismatch is now on record.\n\nCaveat: the catalogue block reached me with its note truncated mid-sentence, so I could not check from here whether this pair is already written down. I propose it anyway and let the executor's run settle novelty; the prediction of movement stands either way.\n\nIf the prototype's own check list is supplied, I will produce one triple of this shape per check: kernel named precisely enough to implement, transform a pure rewrite of the input, input small enough that the before-verdict is obvious.",
